{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788202429,"nonce":"AHW7nXeV8xWECBTmerjCk5/lLvw=","message":"VERIFICATION (backlog verification pass, git-bug 2f2796e, run fbb2c59)\n\nCORRECTED — the body's location line is stale (comment #1 already says so, and\nthe body was never edited), and TWO acceptance criteria depend on a mechanism\nthat does not exist anywhere: there is no bench version.\n\n=====================================================================\nCORRECTION 1 — THERE IS NO BENCH VERSION. TWO ACCEPTANCE ITEMS AND ONE SCOPE\nBULLET DEPEND ON ONE.\n=====================================================================\n\nThe scope says fixtures are \"saves stored under the bench config with\nbench-version tags (DESIGN §Bench); mismatch = hard error offering regen\", the\nscenario format \"declares `bench_version` required\", and the acceptance requires\n\"a bench-version-mismatch scenario ... reported correctly with correct exit\ncodes\".\n\nDESIGN §Bench does say it — \"The bench modlist is versioned; every saved fixture\nis tagged with the bench version it was built on; tier changes =\u003e regenerate\nfixtures\" — but that is the only place the concept appears. A grep for\n`bench_version`, `bench-version` and `benchVersion` across `rwa/`, `profile/` and\n`DESIGN.md` returns **nothing**. Nothing computes a bench version, nothing stamps\none on a save, and nothing stores one. `profile/` holds `make-profile-agent.sh`,\n`gen-modsconfig.py`, `run-agent.sh` and `show-bench.sh` — no versioning step.\n\nSo 5.1 does not CONSUME a bench version, it must DEFINE one, and that is\nunbudgeted work sitting inside an acceptance bullet. It also needs a decision\nthat is not obvious: is the version a hash of the active mod list, a hash of the\nmod list plus each mod's assembly, or a hand-bumped integer? The three differ in\nexactly the case the mechanism exists for — a mod rebuilt with no ModsConfig\nchange, which is the normal state of this workspace. Recommend resolving it\non-issue and, if it grows past a few lines, splitting it out: \"fixtures are\ntagged and mismatches are a hard error\" is a real feature and burying it in a\nrunner spec is how it gets a one-line implementation.\n\n=====================================================================\nCORRECTION 2 — THE BODY STILL SAYS \"PYTHON, IN RIMWORLD-TOOLS\"\n=====================================================================\n\nComment #1 resolves this and says in as many words \"The spec bodies still say\n'lives in rimworld-tools'. Treat this comment as the amendment; do not follow the\nbody on that point.\" Three sessions later the body is unchanged, and it is the\nFIRST line under the title, so a worker reading top-down gets the wrong answer\nbefore it reaches the correction.\n\nCONFIRMED that the resolution is the one in force: `rwa/` exists in this repo\n(`rwa/rwa`, `rwa/fakebench.py`, `rwa/selftest.sh`, `rwa/README.md`), DESIGN's\ndecisions log carries the 2026-08-30 entry, and `rimworld-tools` still has no git\nat all. `rwtest/` does not exist yet — this is fully net-new. Same body-vs-comment\nstaleness as 3.5's acceptance and 4.3's dependency line; three issues now, worth\nfixing in bodies rather than accumulating amendments.\n\n=====================================================================\nCORRECTION 3 — \"ENSURE BENCH RUNNING (BOOT VIA run-agent.sh IF NOT)\" COLLIDES\nWITH THE PROJECT'S HARDEST RULE\n=====================================================================\n\nCLAUDE.md: \"**Workers never launch anything** — the orchestrator runs all in-game\nacceptance personally.\" This spec's Game-lifecycle bullet puts booting the game\ninside the runner, which means the runner cannot be exercised by the agent that\nwrites it, and it means any future agent invoking `rwtest` launches RimWorld.\n\nThat is not necessarily wrong — a CI runner that cannot start its subject is not\nmuch of a runner — but it is a rule the project states in bold and this spec\ncrosses it without noticing. Resolve it explicitly: either the runner refuses to\nboot and reports \"bench not running\" (the orchestrator boots), or booting is an\nexplicitly orchestrator-only flag. Say which, in the spec, with the reason.\n`run-agent.sh` does exist in `profile/`, so the capability is there; the question\nis who is allowed to call it.\n\n=====================================================================\nCONFIRMED\n=====================================================================\n\n- The quarantine context is real. `LogRelay`, `AnalyzerBridge` and\n  `DubsPerformanceAnalyzer` are all on the agent bench\n  (`_RimWorld-Agent/Mods/`), and `rimworld-tools/_meta/` holds the dashboard and\n  scripts the triage queue lives in. \"Do NOT feed it by default\" is a coherent\n  instruction against a thing that exists. CONFIRMED.\n- `no_red_errors` as an implicit default with a loud opt-out matches DESIGN\n  §rwtest verbatim (\"`no_red_errors` is on by default in every test\") and the\n  standing zero-red-errors invariant. CONFIRMED, and the \"opting OUT is loud\"\n  refinement is a genuine addition over DESIGN, worth keeping.\n- \"Property-based within windows, never byte-exact\" matches DESIGN's\n  \"Single-player is not load-deterministic; NEVER assert byte-exact states\".\n  CONFIRMED.\n- \"Run artifacts into RUNS/, never the triage queue\" is consistent with 4.3's\n  `RUNS/m1-\u003cdate\u003e/` deliverable. CONFIRMED, and the two specs should share the\n  directory convention rather than each inventing one.\n\n=====================================================================\nTHINGS THE SPEC DOES NOT SAY THAT IT SHOULD\n=====================================================================\n\n1. **The assertion helpers have a substrate problem the spec does not name.**\n   `eventually_state(query, predicate, window)` requires polling state WHILE time\n   runs — and it cannot. `AgentGameComponent.DrainCommands` fails every non-`pause`\n   command with `Err.Busy` while an advance is in flight. So `eventually_state` is\n   necessarily a loop of `advance` + read, at whatever granularity the advance\n   chunks are, not a continuous watch. That is a real constraint on the helper's\n   semantics and on its cost, and it is the same constraint 1.6 (`fc287ba`) exists\n   to lift — worth a cross-reference, because if `advance until:condition` lands\n   then `eventually_state` becomes a thin wrapper over it instead of a polling\n   loop. Two designs, and which one is right depends on an issue in another wave.\n2. **`rwa/fakebench.py` already exists and is the obvious self-test substrate.**\n   Three of the four acceptance scenarios (passing, failing-assertion,\n   bench-version-mismatch) do not need the game at all — they need a fake bench.\n   The fourth (red-error detection) plausibly does not either, if the fake can\n   emit a journal line. Saying so in the spec turns \"the self-test suite\" from a\n   bench-gated deliverable into a desk one, which matters because this is a p1\n   whose dependencies are all shipped.\n3. **The mod's own `accept/` directory is prior art the spec ignores.** There are\n   already hand-written acceptance scripts there (`accept/32b9e01-orders-makingfor.ps1`\n   is the one I read while verifying `0d9cbd7`), complete with a `Check`/`Note`/\n   `Precondition` vocabulary and per-check ids. Whether `rwtest` subsumes them,\n   replaces them, or ignores them is a real question and the spec does not ask it.\n   My read: the existing scripts encode exactly the \"arrange-act-read\" discipline\n   the runner needs, and at least their check-id and precondition conventions\n   should be lifted rather than re-invented.\n\n=====================================================================\nACCEPTANCE\n=====================================================================\n\n- \"a passing scenario, a failing-assertion scenario, a red-error-detection\n  scenario\" — testable, and probably without the bench (see point 2).\n- \"a bench-version-mismatch scenario\" — **NOT TESTABLE until a bench version\n  exists** (Correction 1). This is the bullet to fix first.\n- \"A worked example test against vanilla behavior ... 'growing zone feeds colony':\n  fixture farm -\u003e advance 3 days -\u003e harvest events + food-days rises\" — testable,\n  needs the bench, and there is a trap in it worth pre-empting: **a growing zone\n  created without an explicit plant grows POTATOES, not nothing.**\n  `RimWorld/Zone_Growing.PlantDefToGrow` lazily assigns and SCRIBES\n  `ThingDefOf.Plant_Potato` on first read. So a fixture that makes a zone and\n  never sets the plant will still produce a harvest — the test passes for the\n  wrong reason. Set the plant in the gen-script and assert on the specific crop.\n  (Same fact is the flagship correction on 4.1 / `96d9315`, from the other\n  direction.)\n  Second trap in the same example: `food_days` is recomputed only every 204 ticks\n  (`RimWorld/ResourceCounter.ResourceCounterTick` is\n  `if (Find.TickManager.TicksGame % 204 == 0)`), so \"food-days rises\" needs a\n  window, not an instant — which is what \"property-based within windows\" already\n  says, but the worked example is the place it will be forgotten.\n\n=====================================================================\nWHAT I AM INFERRING, NOT CONFIRMING\n=====================================================================\n\n- That no bench version exists anywhere. I grepped `rwa/`, `profile/` and\n  `DESIGN.md` for three spellings and read `profile/`'s file list. I did not read\n  every script line by line.\n- That `fakebench.py` can carry three of the four self-test scenarios. I saw the\n  file exists; I did not read it.","files":null}]}